lesson "The Equation of a Line" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Coordinate Geometry"
  difficulty: "Beginner"
  icon: "LineChart"
  summary: "Gradient is rise over run and never the other way round. Slope-intercept, point-slope, and how to build a line's equation from almost nothing."
  skills: ["coord-gradient", "coord-line-equation", "coord-point-slope"]

  slide "Two Numbers Fix a Line" {
    id: "cd2-hook"
    cat: "Gradient and Intercept"
    > A straight line needs exactly two pieces of information: **how steep it is**
    > and **where it sits**. That is the whole content of
    >
    > $$y = mx + c$$
    >
    > $m$ is the **gradient**, how much $y$ climbs for each step of $1$ across. $c$
    > is the **y-intercept**, the height at which it crosses the y-axis.
    >
    > Move both sliders and watch which one tilts the line and which one lifts it.

    scene plane {
      x: [-7, 7]
      y: [-6, 6]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param m = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.25 }
      param c = 1 { range: [-4, 4], step: 0.5 }
      curve line = m*x + c { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point icept = (0, c) { color: accent, r: 8 }
      line runSeg = (1, m + c) -> (2, m + c) { color: warning, width: 4 }
      line riseSeg = (2, m + c) -> (2, 2*m + c) { color: success, width: 4 }
      label at (-6.7, 5.4) = "y = ${m}x + ${c}" { color: primary, show: c >= 0 }
      label at (-6.7, 5.4) = "y = ${m}x - ${0 - c}" { color: primary, show: c < 0 }
      label at (-6.7, 4.5) = "run 1 across, rise ${m} up" { color: neutral }
      slider m { label: "gradient m" }
      slider c { label: "intercept c" }
    }

    goal "Make a line that falls as you go right" {
      when: m < 0
      hint: "A negative gradient tilts it downhill."
    }
    goal "Now make it perfectly flat" {
      when: m == 0
      hint: "No rise at all for any run."
    }
  }

  slide "Rise Over Run, Never the Reverse" {
    id: "cd2-gradient"
    cat: "Gradient and Intercept"
    > From two points, the gradient is
    >
    > $$m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}$$
    >
    > **Rise on top.** Flipping it is the single most common error in this topic, and
    > the way to never do it is to sanity-check against a line you can picture. A
    > steep line climbs a lot for a small step across, so its gradient should be a
    > **big** number. Put the run on top and steep lines would come out near zero,
    > which is obviously wrong.
    >
    > A quick reading of the sign, too: uphill left-to-right is positive, downhill is
    > negative, flat is zero, and a vertical line has no gradient at all, because the
    > run is zero and you cannot divide by it.

    numeric {
      ask "What is the gradient of the line through $(2, 3)$ and $(6, 11)$?"
      skill: "coord-gradient"
      answer: 2
      expect: (11 - 3)/(6 - 2)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Rise is $11 - 3$, run is $6 - 2$. Rise goes on top."
      onwrong: "cd2-rise-over-run" retry
      ! "$m = 8/4 = 2$. It climbs two units for every one across."
    }
  }

  slide "Which Way Up?" {
    id: "cd2-rise-over-run"
    hidden: true
    cat: "Gradient and Intercept"
    > If you got $\tfrac12$, you divided run by rise. Here is a picture that makes
    > the right way round obvious and hard to forget.
    >
    > The blue line below climbs steeply: $4$ up for $1$ across. Its gradient is
    > $4$, a big number for a steep line, which feels right.
    >
    > Flip the fraction and you would call it $\tfrac14$ — a *small* number for a
    > *steep* line. The orange line, which is genuinely shallow, would then also come
    > out large. The two would be the wrong way round entirely.
    >
    > **Steep means big. Steep means lots of rise per run. So rise is on top.**

    scene plane {
      x: [-1, 6]
      y: [-1, 5.5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      curve steep = 4*x - 1 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve shallow = 0.25*x + 0.5 { color: warning, width: 3 }
      line r1 = (1, 3) -> (1.9, 3) { color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line r2 = (1.9, 3) -> (1.9, 6.6) { color: neutral, width: 3 }
      label at (2.0, 4.6) = "steep: m = 4" { color: primary }
      label at (3.4, 1.1) = "shallow: m = 0.25" { color: warning }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "What is the gradient of the line through $(1, 5)$ and $(4, 2)$?"
      skill: "coord-gradient"
      answer: -1
      expect: (2 - 5)/(4 - 1)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Rise is $2 - 5 = -3$, run is $4 - 1 = 3$."
      ! "$m = -3/3 = -1$. It falls as you go right, so a negative gradient is exactly what you should expect."
    }
  }

  slide "When You Do Not Know c" {
    id: "cd2-point-slope"
    cat: "Building the Equation"
    > You usually get a gradient and a point, not a gradient and an intercept. The
    > **point-slope form** handles that directly:
    >
    > $$y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$$
    >
    > It is just the gradient formula rearranged. It says: starting from a point you
    > know, the rise from it is $m$ times the run from it.
    >
    > For gradient $3$ through $(2, 5)$: $\; y - 5 = 3(x - 2)$, which tidies to
    > $y = 3x - 1$.
    >
    > Note you never had to find $c$ separately. It fell out of the tidying, which is
    > exactly why this form is worth using.

    order {
      ask "Put the steps in order to find the equation of the line through $(4, 1)$ with gradient $2$."
      skill: "coord-point-slope"
      item "Write $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$"
      item "Substitute the point and the gradient: $y - 1 = 2(x - 4)$"
      item "Expand the bracket: $y - 1 = 2x - 8$"
      item "Add $1$ to both sides: $y = 2x - 7$"
      decoy "Find $c$ first by guessing where it crosses the y-axis"
      ! "$y = 2x - 7$. Check it against the point: $2(4) - 7 = 1$, which matches. Always substitute the original point back in — it catches sign errors instantly."
    }
  }

  slide "From Two Points" {
    id: "cd2-two-points"
    cat: "Building the Equation"
    > Given two points and nothing else, it is two steps: find the gradient, then
    > use either point in the point-slope form. Either point works and gives the same
    > line, which is a useful thing to check with if you are unsure.

    numeric {
      ask "The line through $(1, 2)$ and $(3, 8)$ is $y = mx + c$. What is $c$?"
      skill: "coord-line-equation"
      answer: -1
      expect: 2 - 3*1
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "First the gradient: $(8-2)/(3-1) = 3$. Then substitute one point into $y = 3x + c$."
      ! "$m = 3$, and $2 = 3(1) + c$ gives $c = -1$, so $y = 3x - 1$. Checking with the other point: $3(3) - 1 = 8$. Both fit, so the line is right."
    }
  }

  slide "Reading a Line Backwards" {
    id: "cd2-read"
    cat: "Building the Equation"
    quiz {
      ask "What is the gradient of the line $2x + 3y = 12$?"
      skill: "coord-gradient"
      * "$-\dfrac{2}{3}$"
      - "$2$" { why: "That is the coefficient of $x$, but only *after* rearranging to $y = mx + c$ does the coefficient mean the gradient. This equation is not in that form yet." }
      - "$\dfrac{2}{3}$" { why: "Right size, wrong sign. Rearranging gives $3y = -2x + 12$, and the minus survives the division." }
      hint "Rearrange it into the form $y = mx + c$ before reading anything off."
      ! "$3y = -2x + 12$, so $y = -\tfrac23 x + 4$. The gradient is $-\tfrac23$ and the intercept is $4$. Never read a gradient off an equation that is not solved for $y$."
    }
  }
}
